A||N||9||Formula88||Clutch - Input Shaft Bearing - Help||||11||
Z||000000||Formula88||02-26-2001||10:14 AM||||Ok, so I'm replacing the clutch in my 88 Formula 5-speed.  Got the tranny out and find out the Input Shaft Bearing sleeve has broken off the bearing.  Here's the bear - that bearing has to be pressed out from inside the tranny case.  Any suggestions on whether a do it yourself-er can successfully split the case?  I don't want to turn my tranny into a do-it-yourself junk pile.  I saw Rodney's directions for splitting the case to swap speedo gears, but it doesn't go into too much detail.  I'm even considering calling some local tranny shops to see what it would cost to have them do it (since I have the part and the tranny is out of the car, that should kill most of the labor).  I'm fairly mechanically inclined, but having never been inside a tranny, it's an unknown, so I'm a bit leary of "learning" on my baby.  Any suggestions, warnings, alternatives?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">||||reg||11
Z||000001||bHooper||02-26-2001||10:21 AM||||i would take it to a tranny shop.  last year, i got a tranny from a corsica, and went about splitting the case, etc.  <P>well to make a short story short, after failing to get it back together, i took it to a tranny shop, and had them install new bearings and reassemble it.<P>it cost me about $270, parts and labor (because i had opened it), and hasn't failed me yet (in 4500 miles)<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||02-26-2001||10:25 AM||||Thanks, but the only problem I have with that is the only transmission shop around Raleigh, NC that I trust, doesn't do manual tranny work.  They weren't even interested when I told them I have the part and the transmission out of the car, I just need you to put in the bearing.  Ugh.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||02-26-2001||12:12 PM||||be patient and call around.  you might have to go outside of raleigh, a bit.  <P>good luck!<P>hoop||||reg||12
Z||000004||88 Database Nut||02-26-2001||01:08 PM||||Hey, all is not lost.  Follow Rodney's instructions (or get them from your 1988 Factory Service Manual) and splitting the case is a snap!  The only tough part is putting the shifter detent balls back in, but even that's not more than a half hour of  fooling around.  I did this job on my 88GT's<BR>tranny several years ago.  Some things to consider. If you don't want to patch and reuse the dust cover (which you remove to split the case), you need to order it from GM before you get into it.  They were available a year ago, but now?  Also, get the I.S. Bearing from them (where else can you get it, the fiero store?), and get a tube of the special goo to seal the tranny from GM (you can use other stuff, if need be).  The bearing was only about $50, the dust cover was maybe $8, and the tube of goo was maybe $10.  You also should carefully check the throwout fork for cracks or wear.  If you have the first design (look for the letters "8ZL" ,I think) stamped in black on the outside of the case to see if you have the first design or second.  The only diff is that the second design has a needle bearing supporting the bottom of the T.O. Fork, where the first desiqn used a bushing.  Removal of the fork is pretty straight forward with Rodney's special tool or a piece of copper pipe union filed/sanded down to go into the housing.  Removal of the lower T.O. Fork bushing is a real Pain though.  Don't ask unless you have to do it.  Check the bottom of the fork for side-to-side play to see if you need to.<P>If you can't see the stamp on the outside of the tranny (it's about halfway between the dipstick and the top), you can sometimes tell by the VIN whether you have an early or late design.  My first GT (1G2PG1190JP207989) was the 7,989 car made in '88 (in Oct/Nov '87) and had the first design.  My second GT (#19,631 produced in (April) '88, had the second design.<P>Good Luck,<P>John Willhoff, Database NUT, (770) 578-6556, BJWillhoff@aol.com, and John.Willhoff@lmco.com (again!).||||reg||1
Z||000005||fieroshop||02-26-2001||03:13 PM||||If you have the bearing then you still need one more part. The part is a black plate that is located just above the bearing that you are replacing. All you do is stick a screwdriver into the palte destroying it and there is a bolt you remove to split the case.You can order this plate from us or your local GM dealer.<P>------------------<BR> <BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieroshop_sig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Archie||02-26-2001||03:16 PM||||If you do it yourself, remember that you'll have to have access to a press to R&R that bearing.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000007||Formula88||02-26-2001||03:26 PM||||Hey, Hoop, do you have any recommendations in the Greensboro area?  I'd rather do it myself than take it to a shop I don't have any references on.  A friend of mine is an import mechanic and he's willing to help, but he's never been inside a tranny similar to the Getrag.  I think I'll probably do it myself, with his help, unless someone can recommend a good shop with in about 100 miles of Raleigh.  Gonna be ordering Rodney's documentation tonight.  Thanks for all the tips, guys!  It's people like you that are gonna make sure Fieros are running well into the 21st century!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000008||PJ||02-26-2001||06:46 PM||||Its not difficult to rebuild these units if you have the factory manual. If you're opening up the case its a good idea to replace both bearings/races on both shafts (imput and output) and especially the diff carrier bearings/races and seals. At least give 'em a close inspection. These bearings are cheap at the local bearing supply house. You can tap the new imput shaft bearing in using a socket and hammer if you're careful. I've rebuilt a few and only needed a press when I dissasembled the shafts themselves. The sealant for the case halves is Loctite 518. You can get it at most auto supply stores. If they only have the small tube you'll need two. E-mail me if you need more info or a copy of relevant shop manual pages.<P> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Formula88||02-26-2001||10:35 PM||||PJ. You've got mail.||||reg||1
